{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"═══ OPERATOR QUESTION ANSWERED: louder error, or deliberate graceful fallback? NEITHER — the gate's\nfailure handling is already correct. The gap is WHERE the error is raised and WHAT it says. ═══\n\n**TRACED, so the fix does not change the wrong thing.** An unresolvable provider is NOT swallowed:\n`coverage.llm_unavailable` → `review_bot/adapter.py:_coverage_gap_reason` returns `\"llm-unavailable\"`\n→ the adapter calls `_block(gap, ...)` → decision **BLOCK** (a `-1` veto), with a message that\nalready separates infrastructure from code:\n\n    rebar code review coverage gap — the review LLM was unavailable (<llm_error>). Fail-closed\n    veto (infrastructure, not your code); re-run once the gate/scanner is healthy.\n\nSo a misconfiguration CANNOT pass silently, and the gate already fails closed. **That behaviour\nshould not change.** Two narrower things should:\n\n**1. FAIL FAST AT CONFIG TIME, not at call time.** Dropping an explicitly configured `provider` is\nnot a runtime outage — it is a CONFIGURATION error, and it is currently reported neither at config\nload nor at resolution. It surfaces ~20 LLM-seconds later, wearing an outage's clothes. A class whose\n`provider` + `model` cannot be composed into a valid target should raise `LLMConfigError` where the\nconfig is read, naming the class, the configured provider, the model id, and the reason.\n\n**2. THE MESSAGE CURRENTLY CONTRADICTS THE OPERATOR'S OWN CONFIG.** What they see is:\n\n    unknown provider 'us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1';\n    registered providers: ['anthropic', 'bedrock']\n\nThey configured `provider = \"bedrock\"`, and the message says `bedrock` IS registered — so it reads as\n\"rebar's Bedrock support is broken\" or \"my credentials are wrong\". The true cause (a colon in the\nmodel id silently voided the provider setting) is unguessable from it. The string it blames is not a\nprovider the operator ever typed; it is most of their model id. Any fix must make the diagnosis name\nthe actual mistake.\n\n**GRACEFUL FALLBACK IS THE WRONG CHOICE HERE — recommending explicitly against it.** Falling back to\n`cfg.model` / a default provider would mean silently reviewing on a DIFFERENT provider than\nconfigured. That is the exact failure this epic exists to remove: its origin was 41 of 42 calls\nsilently reaching direct Anthropic. Bedrock is normally chosen for data-residency, procurement or\ncontractual reasons, so a silent substitution is worse than a loud stop — it produces a cutover that\nlooks complete and is not. Fail closed (already) + fail fast (to add); never fall back.\n\n**CLIENT-CONFUSION ASSESSMENT (the operator's first question): YES, and the likely path is short.**\nThe canonical id is what the AWS console and docs display, so `...-v1:0` is the string an operator\nwill paste. Nothing warns at config load. Reviews then start vetoing every change with an\n\"unavailable\" message that contradicts their config. The cost is misdirected debugging against a\nBLOCKED MERGE QUEUE — the expensive kind of confusion, on the critical path.\n\n**ADDITIONAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA implied by the above** (folding in rather than leaving to be\nre-derived):\n- [ ] a class whose configured `provider` + `model` cannot be composed into a valid target fails at\n      CONFIG-READ time with `LLMConfigError`, naming the class, the provider, the model id and the\n      reason — not at first LLM call\n- [ ] no diagnostic anywhere reports a fragment of a model id as though it were a configured provider\n- [ ] the gate's existing FAIL-CLOSED behaviour on a genuine outage is unchanged and still tested\n      (this ticket must not turn a `-1` veto into a pass)\n- [ ] no silent fallback to `cfg.model` or a default provider is introduced as part of the fix"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785622772620246001,"uuid":"08d6a7b7-556a-4097-895e-1d21a40f5d18"}